WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:03.959
I crawled to the hatch and made my way back to the gallery.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.705
The storm was raging, and no one could stand upright.

3
00:00:07.706 --> 00:00:13.285
Finally at midnight Captain Nemo gave the orders to sink below the waves and escape the storm.

4
00:00:13.286 --> 00:00:20.793
We headed northeast, away from the shores of New York. We were also moving away from Canada, much to Ned's despair.

5
00:00:20.794 --> 00:00:25.356
Like Captain Nemo, he spent most of his time alone during these days.

6
00:00:25.357 --> 00:00:32.713
But Conseil and I stuck together, gazing out the gallery windows and tracing our route on the map as we crossed the Atlantic.

7
00:00:32.714 --> 00:00:40.594
By May 28 we were within 120 miles of Ireland. Ned came out of his cabin and peppered me with questions.

8
00:00:40.595 --> 00:00:43.189
Was the submarine headed for Great Britain?

9
00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:47.189
Did I think Captain Nemo would attempt to sail through the English Channel?

10
00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:53.832
Was he taking us near France? I had no idea what to reply because the captain remained in his cabin. We

11
00:00:53.833 --> 00:00:56.832
could only follow our course on the map. During

12
00:00:56.833 --> 00:01:04.491
the day of May 31, the submarine traveled in circles; it seemed to be seeking a spot that it could not find. I

13
00:01:04.492 --> 00:01:12.650
was up on the platform at noon when Captain Nemo came to take the submarine's bearings—a task his lieutenant usually performed. He

14
00:01:12.651 --> 00:01:16.920
did not say a word to me, but he looked gloomier than ever. What

15
00:01:16.921 --> 00:01:23.054
could make him feel so unhappy? Was it because we were so close to European shores? Did

16
00:01:23.055 --> 00:01:29.324
he have some distressing memories of his abandoned country? Was he feeling remorse or regret? I

17
00:01:29.325 --> 00:01:35.237
was haunted by these questions and had a premonition that the captain's secrets would soon be revealed. The

18
00:01:35.238 --> 00:01:40.975
next day, June 1, the Nautilus continued to hunt for something in the water. Again

19
00:01:40.976 --> 00:01:44.967
it was Captain Nemo who came up to the platform at noon. The

20
00:01:44.968 --> 00:01:53.967
sea was beautiful and the sky was clear. Off to the east I could see a large steamship on the horizon about eight miles away. No

21
00:01:53.968 --> 00:02:01.110
flag fluttered from its mast, so I couldn't tell its country of origin. The captain took the submarine's bearings. "It

22
00:02:01.111 --> 00:02:05.110
is here," he said as he turned and went below. Had

23
00:02:05.111 --> 00:02:11.943
he seen the steamship, which was now changing course and seemed to be heading for us? I could not tell. I

24
00:02:11.944 --> 00:02:15.610
returned to the gallery and the Nautilus began to sink. A

25
00:02:15.611 --> 00:02:21.935
few minutes later, it rested on the sea bottom at a depth of 2,500 feet. I

26
00:02:21.936 --> 00:02:26.253
looked through one of the windows and saw nothing except quiet water. But

27
00:02:26.254 --> 00:02:31.515
through the other window I saw a large object encrusted with white shells. As

28
00:02:31.516 --> 00:02:37.086
I looked closer, I realized that it was a ship that must have sunk a long time ago. "At one

29
00:02:37.087 --> 00:02:44.269
time that ship was called the Marseillais , but it was renamed," said Captain Nemo, who had appeared at my side. "After

30
00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:52.879
a heroic battle, it sank with 356 sailors onboard rather than surrender to the British in 1794. Today

31
00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.007
is the anniversary of its sinking." My

32
00:02:56.008 --> 00:03:02.904
eyes widened as I recalled my French history. "You mean that's the Avenger ?" I exclaimed.

33
00:03:02.905 --> 00:03:10.404
"Yes, the Avenger," said Captain Nemo, stretching out his hand toward the wreck. "A good name!"

34
00:03:10.405 --> 00:03:14.983
My eyes did not leave the captain, who was gazing reverently at the wreck.

35
00:03:14.984 --> 00:03:21.316
I might never know Captain Nemo's real name, where he was from, or where he was going.

36
00:03:21.317 --> 00:03:27.729
But I had just seen him moved by this shipwreck, and that gave me a clue to his character and his past.

37
00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:32.808
It was a strange hatred that had locked Captain Nemo inside the Nautilus with his crew.

38
00:03:32.809 --> 00:03:38.951
And it was a hatred that time could not diminish. I wondered if he still hungered for revenge.

39
00:03:38.952 --> 00:03:44.372
The Nautilus began to slowly rise to the surface, and the Avenger disappeared from view.

40
00:03:44.373 --> 00:03:52.284
Soon a slight rolling motion told me that we were in the open air. At that moment I heard a dull boom.

41
00:03:52.285 --> 00:03:55.594
I looked at the captain, but he did not move.

42
00:03:55.595 --> 00:03:59.514
"Captain!" I said. "What was that sound?"

43
00:03:59.515 --> 00:04:06.260
He did not answer, so I left him and climbed up to the platform. Ned and Conseil were already there.

44
00:04:06.261 --> 00:04:09.347
"Where did that sound come from?" I asked.

45
00:04:09.348 --> 00:04:12.522
"It was cannon fire," replied Ned.

46
00:04:12.523 --> 00:04:22.752
I looked toward the east, where I had last seen that steamship. It was headed straight for us and putting on steam, but it was still miles away.

47
00:04:22.753 --> 00:04:26.077
"What is that ship, Ned?" I asked.

48
00:04:26.078 --> 00:04:36.149
"From its rigging and sails, it looks like a warship," said Ned. "May it reach us, and, if necessary, sink this cursed submarine." "Now,

49
00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:42.990
how will it do that?" asked Conseil. "It can't fire at us if we sink beneath the waves." I

50
00:04:42.991 --> 00:04:46.308
didn't give Ned a chance to respond. "Can

51
00:04:46.309 --> 00:04:50.245
you recognize which country it belongs to?" I asked. Ned

52
00:04:50.246 --> 00:04:54.070
squinted at the ship in the distance for a few moments. "No,

53
00:04:54.071 --> 00:04:59.431
Professor Aronnax. I can't tell where it's from because it's not flying any flag. But

54
00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:04.387
it's definitely a warship because there's a long pennant flying from its mainmast." For

55
00:05:04.388 --> 00:05:09.118
a quarter of an hour, we watched the ship rapidly steaming toward us. Thick

56
00:05:09.119 --> 00:05:12.880
black smoke was pouring from its two funnels. I

57
00:05:12.881 --> 00:05:19.896
had a hard time believing that anyone could see the Nautilus from that distance, but the ship did seem focused on us. "Professor,"

58
00:05:19.897 --> 00:05:33.626
said Ned, "if that vessel passes within a mile of us, I'm going to throw myself into the sea, and I advise you two to do the same. It will pick us up and we'll be off this wretched submarine for good." I

59
00:05:33.627 --> 00:05:38.785
didn't reply to Ned's suggestion but continued to watch the ship approach. "Master,

60
00:05:38.786 --> 00:05:44.856
you may recall that we have some experience with swimming," said Conseil. "Yes,

61
00:05:44.857 --> 00:05:47.245
Conseil, I remember that—" I

62
00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:51.936
stopped speaking as a puff of white smoke burst from the bow of the ship. A

63
00:05:51.937 --> 00:05:59.674
few seconds later, something heavy made a big splash near the submarine's stern, and I heard a loud explosion. "What?

64
00:05:59.675 --> 00:06:06.015
They're firing at us!" I exclaimed. "Surely they can see that there are men onboard." "That

65
00:06:06.016 --> 00:06:10.150
may be exactly why they're firing on us." Ned looked at me. And

66
00:06:10.151 --> 00:06:13.484
suddenly I understood what was happening. I

67
00:06:13.485 --> 00:06:19.825
thought back to November when we were hunting the giant narwhal onboard the Abraham Lincoln . When

68
00:06:19.826 --> 00:06:29.071
Ned's harpoon hit the Nautilus , Captain Farragut must have realized that he was dealing with a dangerous submarine and not a sea creature.

69
00:06:29.072 --> 00:06:32.317
He obviously hadn't kept that news to himself.

70
00:06:32.318 --> 00:06:37.809
Ships had probably been crisscrossing the oceans in search of this underwater vessel ever since.

71
00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:44.214
To my horror, I also realized that Captain Nemo had used the Nautilus as an instrument of revenge.

72
00:06:44.215 --> 00:06:50.706
On the night when he'd locked us in that cell and drugged our food, the submarine must have attacked another ship.

73
00:06:50.707 --> 00:06:54.888
The man buried in the coral cemetery must have died in that battle.

74
00:06:54.889 --> 00:06:58.619
Someone knew who Captain Nemo really was.

75
00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:05.705
Someone knew that he was out to avenge some horrible wrong, and now whole nations were hunting him down!

76
00:07:05.706 --> 00:07:14.683
I faced the grim truth. Instead of meeting friends onboard this approaching ship, we could only expect merciless enemies.

